      "text": "To find the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to ensure that there exists some \\( x \\) such that \\( f(x) = -2 \\). This means we need to solve the equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c = -2\n\\]\n\nRearrange the equation to form a standard quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis can be written as:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0\n\\]\n\nFor \\( x \\) to be a real number, the discriminant of this quadratic equation must be non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + d = 0 \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ad\n\\]\n\nIn our equation, \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( d = c + 2 \\). Therefore, the discriminant is:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (c + 2)\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 9 - 4(c + 2)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 9 - 4c - 8\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 1 - 4c\n\\]\n\nFor \\( x \\) to be real, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n\n\\[\n1 - 4c \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\( c \\):\n\n\\[\n1 \\geq 4c\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\geq c\n\\]\n\n\\[\nc \\leq \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n\\]",
