      "text": "To determine the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to find the value of \\( c \\) for which there exists an \\( x \\) such that \\( f(x) = -2 \\).\n\nFirst, we set the function equal to \\(-2\\):\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c = -2\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\( c \\):\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c = -2\n\\]\n\nRearrange the equation to isolate \\( c \\):\n\\[\nc = -2 - x^2 - 3x\n\\]\n\nTo maximize \\( c \\), we need to find the minimum value of \\( x^2 + 3x \\). The function \\( x^2 + 3x \\) is a quadratic function, which opens upwards (since the coefficient of \\( x^2 \\) is positive). Therefore, its minimum value occurs at the vertex.\n\nThe vertex of a quadratic function \\( ax^2 + bx + c \\) occurs at \\( x = -\\frac{b}{2a} \\). Here, \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = 3 \\), so:\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{3}{2 \\cdot 1} = -\\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\) back into the expression \\( x^2 + 3x \\):\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x = \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2 + 3\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{9}{4} - \\frac{9}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{9}{4} - \\frac{18}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\n= -\\frac{9}{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\( x^2 + 3x \\) is \\( -\\frac{9}{4} \\). Therefore, the maximum value of \\( c \\) is:\n\\[\nc = -2 - \\left(-\\frac{9}{4}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n= -2 + \\frac{9}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\n= -\\frac{8}{4} + \\frac{9}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nHence, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n\\]",
